1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,725
Kampong Lorong Buangkok: The
Last Kampong on the Mainland

2
00:00:04,725 --> 00:00:06,400
It survived.

3
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:10,078
Every other kampong on
the mainland is gone,

4
00:00:10,158 --> 00:00:13,835
but this one is still
here — and as of 2024,

5
00:00:13,915 --> 00:00:18,100
the rent for some of its
houses is $6.50 a month.

6
00:00:18,100 --> 00:00:21,725
The most anyone pays is $30.

7
00:00:21,725 --> 00:00:25,639
Those figures have
barely moved in decades —

8
00:00:25,719 --> 00:00:29,996
the same $6.50 to $30
range reported in 2007 and

9
00:00:30,076 --> 00:00:34,535
again in 2024 — and if a
tenant is short of money,

10
00:00:34,615 --> 00:00:38,700
the landlady will take
rice or fruit instead.

11
00:00:38,700 --> 00:00:42,406
There are twenty-five families
left — twelve Chinese,

12
00:00:42,486 --> 00:00:46,406
thirteen Malay — living in
wooden houses with zinc roofs

13
00:00:46,486 --> 00:00:50,700
along earth lanes, behind the
HDB blocks of Buangkok Green.

14
00:00:50,700 --> 00:00:54,704
The landlady is Sng Mui Hong,
who was seventy-one when

15
00:00:54,784 --> 00:00:58,864
CNA profiled her in November
2024, and who has promised

16
00:00:58,944 --> 00:01:02,725
her late father that the
kampong will not be sold.

17
00:01:02,725 --> 00:01:07,400
"As long as I have enough to live,
that's already fine," she said.

18
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:10,250
"There's no need to be greedy.

19
00:01:10,250 --> 00:01:14,875
Even if I get a lot of money, it
will all be finished eventually."

20
00:01:14,875 --> 00:01:18,233
This is Kampong Lorong
Buangkok, the last kampong

21
00:01:18,313 --> 00:01:21,812
on mainland Singapore — a
village that was supposed

22
00:01:21,892 --> 00:01:25,050
to disappear with all
the others, and didn't.

23
00:01:25,050 --> 00:01:28,467
The land — 12,248 square metres of it,

24
00:01:28,547 --> 00:01:33,529
roughly three football fields
— was swamp when Sng Teow

25
00:01:33,609 --> 00:01:37,750
Koon bought it in 1956
from a Mr Huang Yu Tu.

26
00:01:37,750 --> 00:01:42,100
Sng sold traditional Chinese
medicine for a living.

27
00:01:42,100 --> 00:01:47,090
Four to six houses already stood on
the plot; he moved his own family

28
00:01:47,170 --> 00:01:51,800
in and began renting out parcels
to other families to build on.

29
00:01:51,800 --> 00:01:55,596
In the earliest years there were
just five or six households,

30
00:01:55,676 --> 00:01:59,408
Malay and Chinese, and the first
work was the ground itself:

31
00:01:59,488 --> 00:02:03,728
residents hauled sand from the
nearby estuary to fill the mud before

32
00:02:03,808 --> 00:02:07,604
they could build, and built each
other's houses in the gotong

33
00:02:07,684 --> 00:02:11,750
royong way — your neighbours raise
your walls, you raise theirs.

34
00:02:11,750 --> 00:02:16,024
Many of the first tenants worked
close by, at Woodbridge Hospital

35
00:02:16,104 --> 00:02:19,574
(today's Institute of Mental
Health) or on the rubber

36
00:02:19,654 --> 00:02:24,075
plantations of the district, and
their descendants are still here.

37
00:02:24,075 --> 00:02:26,738
By the 1960s the kampong held

38
00:02:26,818 --> 00:02:29,750
about forty families, its peak.

39
00:02:29,750 --> 00:02:34,950
Running water and
electricity arrived in 1962.

40
00:02:34,950 --> 00:02:39,062
It also had a second name:
Kampong Selak Kain — selak kain,

41
00:02:39,142 --> 00:02:43,893
"lift up your sarong" — because the
low ground beside Sungei Punggol

42
00:02:43,973 --> 00:02:48,875
flooded so readily that residents
hitched up their sarongs and waded.

43
00:02:48,875 --> 00:02:52,883
The floods never fully left;
in March 2004 the kampong

44
00:02:52,963 --> 00:02:57,122
made headlines when heavy
rain put it under water again,

45
00:02:57,202 --> 00:03:00,226
and officials judged full flood defences,

46
00:03:00,306 --> 00:03:05,075
costed at up to $10 million,
impractical for a private village.

47
00:03:05,075 --> 00:03:08,450
Volunteers fenced the stream instead.

48
00:03:08,450 --> 00:03:12,521
The village's prayer
house, Surau Al-Firdaus,

49
00:03:12,601 --> 00:03:17,225
was first built in 1953 —
before the Sng purchase —

50
00:03:17,305 --> 00:03:23,300
and rebuilt in 1967 and again in
1999, with a renovation in 2018.

51
00:03:23,300 --> 00:03:27,063
It still draws worshippers
from outside the kampong as

52
00:03:27,143 --> 00:03:31,200
well as the families within,
and holds religious classes.

53
00:03:31,200 --> 00:03:36,314
In 2004 the village headman, Awe Bin
Ludin, looked after Muslim affairs —

54
00:03:36,394 --> 00:03:40,228
the surau, the prayer times,
the Ramadan announcements,

55
00:03:40,308 --> 00:03:44,150
the collections for repairs
and for bereaved families.

56
00:03:44,150 --> 00:03:48,051
Around the houses run the
details every visitor remembers:

57
00:03:48,131 --> 00:03:52,101
chickens, cats and dogs roaming
loose, jackfruit and banana

58
00:03:52,181 --> 00:03:56,563
and chilli and lime in the gardens,
dirt tracks instead of roads,

59
00:03:56,643 --> 00:04:00,350
and old street signs carrying
four-digit postal codes.

60
00:04:00,350 --> 00:04:04,232
One kept sign reads
"Lorong Buang Kok 1954" —

61
00:04:04,312 --> 00:04:09,603
visitors constantly take 1954
for the year the kampong began,

62
00:04:09,683 --> 00:04:12,325
but it is the old postal code.

63
00:04:12,325 --> 00:04:16,875
The village is younger than its
sign, and prouder of other dates.

64
00:04:16,875 --> 00:04:21,821
Singapore spent the second half
of the twentieth century rehousing

65
00:04:21,901 --> 00:04:26,850
a nation — the kampongs went, one
by one, into the HDB new towns.

66
00:04:26,850 --> 00:04:29,995
Lorong Buangkok survived
the great clearing

67
00:04:30,075 --> 00:04:33,150
for a plain legal reason and a human one.

68
00:04:33,150 --> 00:04:35,920
The legal reason: the land was private,

69
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:39,800
bought and paid for, not
state land awaiting a plan.

70
00:04:39,800 --> 00:04:43,475
The human reason is the
family that owned it.

71
00:04:43,475 --> 00:04:45,837
Sng Teow Koon died in 1997,

72
00:04:45,917 --> 00:04:49,625
and the land passed to his four children.

73
00:04:49,625 --> 00:04:52,962
The three elder siblings
moved out to flats;

74
00:04:53,042 --> 00:04:55,450
the youngest, Mui Hong, stayed.

75
00:04:55,450 --> 00:04:59,402
She had promised her father
she would preserve the kampong,

76
00:04:59,482 --> 00:05:01,600
and she runs it the way he did.

77
00:05:01,600 --> 00:05:05,995
The rents stay where they
were — in 2007, the total she

78
00:05:06,075 --> 00:05:10,550
collected from every tenant
came to about $300 a month.

79
00:05:10,550 --> 00:05:14,464
Nobody new has moved in since
the 1990s: a house falls

80
00:05:14,544 --> 00:05:19,051
empty only when someone leaves
or dies, and then it is offered

81
00:05:19,131 --> 00:05:23,125
only to people connected to
the village or the family.

82
00:05:23,125 --> 00:05:25,146
The tenants own their houses;

83
00:05:25,226 --> 00:05:28,125
what they rent is the ground under them.

84
00:05:28,125 --> 00:05:32,875
In 2007 the land was reported
to be valued at $33 million,

85
00:05:32,955 --> 00:05:36,789
and the New Paper put
her answer in a headline:

86
00:05:36,869 --> 00:05:39,700
"My family ties are not for sale."

87
00:05:39,700 --> 00:05:44,073
She said it herself, the same year:
"I can never regain this feeling

88
00:05:44,153 --> 00:05:48,475
of simplicity and freedom, and be
close to my childhood memories."

89
00:05:48,475 --> 00:05:51,651
Two years later, when the
New York Times came to write

90
00:05:51,731 --> 00:05:55,087
about Singapore's last village,
she explained the village

91
00:05:55,167 --> 00:05:58,704
she was keeping: "I have a deep
attachment to my neighbours…

92
00:05:58,784 --> 00:06:01,538
After all they grew up
with me, and some of the

93
00:06:01,618 --> 00:06:05,175
grandmothers and grandfathers
here have watched me grow up.

94
00:06:05,175 --> 00:06:09,625
They are like my own parents because
we are from the same village.

95
00:06:09,625 --> 00:06:13,150
So I don't care if they
are Malay or Chinese."

96
00:06:13,150 --> 00:06:16,475
Fame arrived slowly, then all at once.

97
00:06:16,475 --> 00:06:20,671
The 2004 flood put the
kampong in the papers;

98
00:06:20,751 --> 00:06:25,898
the 2007 valuation made it
a story about money refused;

99
00:06:25,978 --> 00:06:31,775
the Times made it world-famous
in 2009; the BBC came in 2021.

100
00:06:31,775 --> 00:06:36,400
Documentary crews, school groups
and weekend walkers followed.

101
00:06:36,400 --> 00:06:40,481
Guided tours — the sanctioned
way in — fill to capacity,

102
00:06:40,561 --> 00:06:45,311
and since 2025 a Singapore Culture
Pass tour has brought visitors

103
00:06:45,391 --> 00:06:49,700
down the earth track to meet
Sng Mui Hong at her own home.

104
00:06:49,700 --> 00:06:53,625
The attention has a price,
and the residents pay it.

105
00:06:53,625 --> 00:06:57,474
Travel writers who once
described gates left open now

106
00:06:57,554 --> 00:07:01,995
describe fences between plots,
doors locked, curtains drawn —

107
00:07:02,075 --> 00:07:06,300
the village defending its
privacy from its own celebrity.

108
00:07:06,300 --> 00:07:10,300
It is private residential land,
not a museum: the lanes are

109
00:07:10,380 --> 00:07:14,311
open to a quiet walker, but
the houses are people's homes,

110
00:07:14,391 --> 00:07:18,391
several of them elderly people's
homes, and the tours exist

111
00:07:18,471 --> 00:07:22,275
partly so that visiting
happens on the village's terms.

112
00:07:22,275 --> 00:07:26,425
On paper, the kampong's
future is already drawn.

113
00:07:26,425 --> 00:07:30,533
The site is divided in the
Master Plan into four parcels:

114
00:07:30,613 --> 00:07:33,912
a major road toward
Seletar, a primary school,

115
00:07:33,992 --> 00:07:37,225
a secondary school and
a neighbourhood park.

116
00:07:37,225 --> 00:07:40,132
When the 2014 Master Plan set that out,

117
00:07:40,212 --> 00:07:44,500
there were public calls to
conserve the village instead.

118
00:07:44,500 --> 00:07:49,165
In Parliament on the 2nd of October
2017, MP Intan Azura Mokhtar

119
00:07:49,245 --> 00:07:54,578
asked for its preservation; the Second
Minister for National Development,

120
00:07:54,658 --> 00:07:59,175
Desmond Lee, replied that
redevelopment would likely come only

121
00:07:59,255 --> 00:08:04,075
"several decades later," tied to
the long-term growth of Seletar.

122
00:08:04,075 --> 00:08:07,676
The kampong has no
conservation status in law —

123
00:08:07,756 --> 00:08:11,750
its protection is, and has
always been, its owners.

124
00:08:11,750 --> 00:08:14,025
The residents took the minister's

125
00:08:14,105 --> 00:08:16,675
words the way they take most things.

126
00:08:16,675 --> 00:08:20,961
Mohamed Sulaiman, a long-time
resident, said at the time:

127
00:08:21,041 --> 00:08:25,100
"If government say, can
stay, 20 year – I very happy.

128
00:08:25,100 --> 00:08:28,712
I don't know I where,
100 over years already,

129
00:08:28,792 --> 00:08:30,925
but if can stay, I happy."

130
00:08:30,925 --> 00:08:35,678
So the village goes on: twenty-five
families, the surau's green roof,

131
00:08:35,758 --> 00:08:40,230
the mango trees that Sng Mui Hong
talks to when her mood is bad —

132
00:08:40,310 --> 00:08:44,222
"When my mood isn't good, I
can talk to its mango trees,"

133
00:08:44,302 --> 00:08:48,925
she told CNA — and a rent book
that would have made sense in 1986.

134
00:08:48,925 --> 00:08:52,174
Her advice, at seventy-one,
was the kampong's

135
00:08:52,254 --> 00:08:56,100
philosophy in one breath:
"Don't think so far ahead.

136
00:08:56,100 --> 00:08:59,900
The more you think, the
worse it is for your brain.

137
00:08:59,900 --> 00:09:01,927
Just be a bit more open-minded,

138
00:09:02,007 --> 00:09:04,725
then you will have no problems in life."

139
00:09:04,725 --> 00:09:08,988
Where it fits in the bigger story:
Singapore's kampongs were not

140
00:09:09,068 --> 00:09:13,750
preserved by policy; they were
cleared by it, almost to the last one.

141
00:09:13,750 --> 00:09:16,864
This one survives
because a medicine seller

142
00:09:16,944 --> 00:09:20,429
bought a swamp in 1956,
and because his youngest

143
00:09:20,509 --> 00:09:23,925
daughter decided that
enough money was enough.

144
00:09:23,925 --> 00:09:28,027
When she is asked what the land
is worth, she says she has had no

145
00:09:28,107 --> 00:09:32,338
direct offers and does not know or
care — the most unusual sentence

146
00:09:32,418 --> 00:09:36,327
in Singapore property, and the
reason a whole village is still

147
00:09:36,407 --> 00:09:40,525
standing at the end of an earth
track behind the Buangkok flats.

148
00:09:40,525 --> 00:09:42,675
A note of my own.

149
00:09:42,675 --> 00:09:46,722
I grew up in Aukang, and most
of my life was lived within

150
00:09:46,802 --> 00:09:50,415
a few streets of this
kampong - in Jalan Payoh Lai,

151
00:09:50,495 --> 00:09:54,253
in Jalan Teliti, in Jalan
Tari Piring off Jalan Kayu,

152
00:09:54,333 --> 00:09:58,750
and in a rented house in Jalan
Sindor, off Yio Chu Kang Road.

153
00:09:58,750 --> 00:10:02,258
I never thought of Buangkok
as the last kampong,

154
00:10:02,338 --> 00:10:06,375
or even as a Malay village;
it was just a road I knew.

155
00:10:06,375 --> 00:10:09,178
To a boy, the distance from Aukang to

156
00:10:09,258 --> 00:10:12,375
Jalan Kayu seemed too much ever to walk.

157
00:10:12,375 --> 00:10:15,485
It is about five miles
- no more than the walk

158
00:10:15,565 --> 00:10:18,825
I now take around my
neighbourhood in Maryland.

159
00:10:18,825 --> 00:10:22,550
On my next visit home,
I intend to walk it.
